  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-138836",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{({x}^{2}-2x-8)}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x-8)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\cdot (2x-2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}(2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}(\\frac{2x({x}^{2}-2x-8)-(4x-4)}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}(2x({x}^{2}-2x-8)-(4x-4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}(2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-16x-4x+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x+2)}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x+2.\n$$",
